(** JavaScript, for a program that cesia build --target wasm compiled: it runs in a page or in Node, and Call calls a
    function of a JavaScript module beside it. That is the one way out; the one way in is a channel given to Call, which
    JavaScript fills. cesia run has no JavaScript: there, Call is a bug. *)
use std/bug

defn Call(module, name, args)
  (** Calls the function name of the JavaScript module module, both texts written in the call: a path beside the
      program, as "./ceres.js", or "cesia:websocket", the loader's own. args is a list, each argument given to the
      function as JavaScript takes it: a text as a Uint8Array of its bytes, a number as a number, a boolean as a boolean,
      missing as null, a list as an array, and a channel of std/fiber as a handle whose send(value) puts a value in it
      and close() closes it, which JavaScript keeps to send values later, as events come. What the function gives comes
      back the same way: a string as its UTF-8, bytes as a text, a number as an integer when it is whole, null or
      undefined as missing, an array as a list. Anything else, a dictionary or a function, is an error.

        events = fiber.Channel()
        width = js.Call("./ceres.js", "Measure", ["Invoices", 14, "body"])
        js.Call("./ceres.js", "Start", [events])
        fiber.Receive(events) *)
  bug.Panic("js.Call($module, $name) runs only in a program that cesia build --target wasm compiled, in a page or in Node.")
end
